Two KINDS of strand — keeper vs development (from ubersitrep 2026-08-02).
THEORY of the strand system → belongs to this (strands) strand. The PRACTICAL
build (templates, tooling) → aifabric. (Boundary, Peter 2026-08-02: "aifabric
is practical stranding, strands is strand theory.")

THE THEORY (this strand owns):
- KEEPER strand = steady-state custodian of a running thing: repetitive,
  frequently visited on a CADENCE (not to finish something), specialized
  (narrow/deep — one camera, the data plumbing). Modifies STATE SPARINGLY —
  STATE is the *stable spec of a healthy system*, not a worklog; it changes
  when the system's shape changes (a new calibration constant, a config
  decision), not every visit. Each keeper DEFINES METRICS for how well it's
  performing (captured last night? frame count, focus quality, GB/night, disk
  headroom) — a keeper without metrics is just a dormant strand. Reviewed by
  cadence + a metrics-reading scheduled agent.
- DEVELOPMENT strand = progresses a TASK toward done: goal-directed, STATE is
  the moving frontier updated every session (the dcp worklog: moved/next/
  blocked), measured by PROGRESS. Reviewed by the least-recently-reviewed
  backlog rotation.
- The conceptual consequences to work out HERE: is "kind" a spectrum or binary?
  can a strand switch kind (dev → keeper once the thing it built is running)?
  does every keeper truly need metrics, or only ones feeding a sitrep? how does
  kind interact with the review ledger vs a cadence schedule?

THE PRACTICAL BUILD (hand to aifabric — practical stranding):
- .template keeper vs dev variant (keeper scaffold carries a METRICS block; dev
  scaffold carries the moving-frontier STATE shape).
- a declared-kind field so tools (the sitrep agent, the ledger) treat keepers
  and dev strands differently.
- README/method-doc documenting the two kinds + review discipline each gets.

First live instance: the astro re-org — polecam/eclipticam/canon/storage are
keepers; astro-science is development. See ubersitrep STATE for that application.
